# Project Development Rules

## Context first
- This repo is **BOGCL OneOps**. The active deliverable is the **Flutter pitch app in `ftr-a/`**: no backend, seeded mock data, but it must look and behave like a real production app. `client/` (web prototype) and `app/` (React Native) are read-only references; `server/` and `frontend/` are not being built. Read `AGENTS.md` and `ftr-a/docs/FEATURE_STATUS.md` before non-trivial work.
- Business rules live in `docs/platform/06_MODULE_FUNCTIONAL_SPECIFICATIONS.md`; mobile spec in `docs/platform/05_MOBILE_APP_SPECIFICATION_FLUTTER.md`; API shapes in `03` (the mock mirrors them). Implement from the docs; when behaviour must change, update the doc in the same change.

## General
- Act as a senior software engineer. Understand the existing architecture before making changes.
- Follow existing project conventions. Prefer simple, maintainable solutions; avoid overengineering.
- Do not duplicate existing functionality (check engines in `server/src/engines` and shared UI first).

## Dart / TypeScript
- Dart: sound null safety, strict analyzer (`strict-casts/inference/raw-types`), no `dynamic` unless unavoidable, immutable models.
- TypeScript (legacy apps only): strict; avoid `any`.

## Code Quality
- Keep functions focused and readable; handle loading, error, empty, and success states.
- Consider edge cases; remove unused imports and code.
- Never hardcode user-facing text — use i18n keys (EN + BN; ARB files in `ftr-a/lib/l10n`).

## Dependencies
- Reuse existing dependencies. Do not install new packages without explaining why.
- Never replace an existing library without approval.

## Safety
- Never expose API keys, tokens, passwords, or secrets. `.env.example` files contain names only.
- Never overwrite environment files.
- Do not run destructive database commands without approval.
- Do not modify production configuration without approval.

## Workflow
- Inspect relevant files before editing. For complex tasks, create a plan first.
- Keep changes focused on the requested task.
- Run relevant tests, lint, and type checks; explain what changed and report verification results.
